# Migration skill — bugs and gaps

Found scaffolding `tournewzealand.com`, the second site through
`webflow-sanity:migration`. Everything here cost time on this migration and will cost it again
on the next one. Ordered by what hurt most.

The skill is at `~/.claude/skills/webflow-sanity/skills/migration/` — `SKILL.md`,
`references/scaffold.md`, `templates/` (6 files), `migration-scripts/` (the engine).

## 1. The scaffold ships no files — only prose

**The single biggest gap.** §2 says *"Copy the config from a finished migration rather than
writing it"*, and `references/scaffold.md` describes the root scripts, the Wrangler configs, the
JSONC readers and the acceptance checks — **in prose, with nothing to copy.** `templates/`
contains six files and not one of them is a scaffold file.

So §2 is unactionable as written, and every file below had to be authored from scratch, from
prose, with the mistakes that implies:

| Area | Files the skill does not ship |
|---|---|
| Root | `package.json`, `.env.example`, `.gitignore` entries, `wrangler.jsonc`, `wrangler.prod.jsonc` |
| Config readers | `config/parse-project.mjs`, `config/read-project.mjs`, `config/read-project.cjs` |
| Schema | `schema/index.mjs` |
| Site | `web/package.json`, `web/astro.config.mjs`, `web/tsconfig.json`, `web/src/env.d.ts`, `web/src/lib/project.ts`, `web/src/lib/sanity.ts`, `web/src/lib/assets.ts`, `web/public/.assetsignore` |
| Studio | `studio/package.json`, `studio/sanity.config.ts`, `studio/sanity.cli.ts`, `studio/tsconfig.json`, `studio/project-config.ts`, `studio/types/raw-modules.d.ts`, `studio/.env.example` |

**Fix:** ship all of them under `templates/scaffold/`, mirroring the repo layout, and give §2 a
`cp -r` block exactly like the one §0 already has for `migration-scripts/`. Prose describing a
file is not a template.

**This is also why Phase 1 came out incomplete here.** Without the files, "set up the initial
Sanity configuration" has no concrete deliverable, so there was nowhere for the PM to put the
project values and no Studio to point at.

## 2. The engine's own dependencies are undeclared

`migration-scripts/` imports `jsdom` and `jsonc-parser`. Nothing in the skill says so. The
symptom is `npm test` and `npm run sanity:verify` dying with:

```
Error [ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND]: Cannot find package 'jsdom' imported from
  migration-scripts/sanity/html-to-portable-text.mjs
```

**Fix:** name them in §0 beside the `cp -r`, and have them in the shipped root `package.json`.

## 3. §2 leads with a layout the engine does not support

`scaffold.md` opens with *"For an Astro app at the repository root, merge these entries into
package.json"* and treats `web/` as the variant. But **every** engine script hardcodes it:

```js
const WEB = join(ROOT, 'web');
```

`extract-webflow-page.mjs`, `fetch-webflow-assets.mjs`, `localise-webflow-assets.mjs`,
`check-assets.mjs`, `verify-port.mjs`, `check-sitemap.mjs`, `gen-redirects.mjs` — all of them.
Follow the documented primary shape and the engine writes into a `web/` that does not exist,
silently, until a later step cannot find `pages.json`.

**Fix:** state `web/` + `studio/` + root orchestration as **the** layout, or read the app
directory from `project.jsonc`. Do not present a choice the engine cannot honour.

## 4. The doc and the only worked example disagree on where Wrangler configs live

`scaffold.md`: *"keep deploy orchestration and both Wrangler configs at the repository root."*
The reference migration (`sdm`) has them in `web/`.

Root is the better answer — `wrangler` then installs once at the root and
`"$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json"` resolves — but the contradiction has to
go.

## 5. The Cloudflare output layout in §8 is stale

§8 and `scaffold.md` prescribe:

```jsonc
"main": "./dist/_worker.js/index.js",
"assets": {"directory": "./dist", ...}
```

and a `public/.assetsignore` containing `_worker.js` and `_routes.json`.

With **Astro 7 + `@astrojs/cloudflare` 14**, a static build emits none of that:

| Prescribed | Actual |
|---|---|
| `dist/_worker.js/index.js` | does not exist |
| `dist/_routes.json` | does not exist |
| `assets.directory: ./dist` | the site is in **`dist/client/`** |
| — | `dist/server/` exists and is **empty** when everything prerenders |
| — | the adapter writes its own **`dist/client/wrangler.json`** declaring a SESSION KV and an IMAGES binding |

For a fully-prerendered site the correct production config has **no `main` at all** — it is an
assets-only deployment. Copying the prescribed shape yields a Worker entry pointing at a file
that was never written.

**Fix:** stop prescribing paths. Tell the reader to build first and read what the adapter
actually emitted, document the v14 layout, and spell out what changes the moment one route sets
`prerender = false` — add `main` (the adapter's real server entry, read not guessed), drop
`not_found_handling`, and carry over the adapter's bindings.

## 6. `not_found_handling` guidance points at a file that no longer exists

The §8 decision table is still correct in substance, but its verification step is
`cat dist/_routes.json` — which v14 does not produce. The check is now
`grep -rn "prerender = false" web/src/` plus reading `web/dist/client/wrangler.json`.

## 7. Reading `project.jsonc` from site code breaks the prerender

`scaffold.md` says to *"use an ESM filesystem reader anchored to the root command's working
directory"* and warns that *"Astro may relocate bundled modules."* That is not enough to act on.
What actually happens when a page imports the ESM reader:

```
TypeError: The "path" argument must be of type string or an instance of URL. Received undefined
    at fileURLToPath (node-internal:internal_url)
    at web/dist/server/.prerender/chunks/index_DoNnIZDQ.mjs:776
```

`import.meta.url` is `undefined` once Astro bundles the module into a hashed prerender chunk, and
the stack frame points into that chunk rather than at the cause.

**The pattern that works:** resolve `project.jsonc` once in `astro.config.mjs` — plain Node,
never bundled — and inject it with Vite `define`. No filesystem reader ever enters the bundle, so
the failure is structurally impossible rather than merely fixed.

**Fix:** prescribe that, and ship `web/astro.config.mjs` + `web/src/lib/project.ts` as templates.

## 8. The `define` pattern has two traps of its own

Both cost a build cycle here, and both will recur, so they belong in the doc alongside item 7.

> [!WARNING]
> **A local `declare const` is erased along with the export that uses it.** esbuild treats
> `declare const __PROJECT_CONFIG__` as a type-only binding and strips the `export default` that
> references it, failing with the thoroughly misleading
> `[MISSING_EXPORT] "default" is not exported by "src/lib/project.ts"`. Declare it as a
> `declare global { var … }` instead.

> [!WARNING]
> **Vite `define` replaces the BARE identifier only.** `globalThis.__PROJECT_CONFIG__` is a
> member expression, is never matched, and is `undefined` at render time **with no build error
> at all** — the worst available failure mode.

## 9. A fourth asset-URL trap, missing from §5

§5 documents the HTML-vs-CSS terminator and the parentheses-in-filenames trap. There is a third
form on this site: Webflow writes inline backgrounds as

```html
style="background-image:url(&quot;https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/…&quot;)"
```

A scanner that does not **decode entities before matching** runs past the closing `&quot;` and
captures a URL ending in `&quot;)`. Webflow answers those with **403**, so they present as dead
assets rather than as the local bug they are — 127 of them on the first pass here.

**Fix:** add to §5's trap table — decode entities first, *then* apply the per-context terminator.

## 10. There is no definition of "the scaffold is done"

§9's checklist is cut-over oriented. `scaffold.md`'s acceptance list is all commands and no
files, so a scaffold can pass every listed check while the Studio does not exist — which is
exactly what happened.

**Fix:** a §2 acceptance list of **files that must exist**, followed by the commands. Phase 1 is
complete when the PM can fill in two env files and nothing else is missing.

## 11. Smaller things

- **Per-site templates crash instead of refusing.** `templates/verify.mjs` and
  `templates/collections.mjs` ship with `<parentCollection>` placeholders, so `npm run
  sanity:verify` dies with `ENOENT: migration/raw/<parentCollection>.json`. The fixtures check
  gets this right — *"Create it and add verbatim excerpts…"* — and these should match it.
- **npm 11 blocks postinstall scripts by default.** `esbuild` and `workerd` are gated, so
  `wrangler` has no binary until `npm install-scripts approve esbuild workerd` is run, in each
  package that depends on them. Worth one line in §2.
- **`.env` location is load-bearing and only explained in the reference repo's comments.** With
  the app in `web/`, `.env` stays at the ROOT and `web/astro.config.mjs` needs
  `vite.envDir: '..'`, aligned with the guard's `--env-dir`. Get it wrong and the build succeeds
  while every query reads the wrong dataset. `scaffold.md` mentions the setting but not the
  consequence.
- **`studioHost` availability is not verifiable up front.** `navi.sanity.studio` already 302s to
  a studio under another org id, and `sanity deploy --dry-run` only says *"name availability is
  checked on deploy"*. Worth a §2 note to check the hostname early, since it cannot be confirmed
  without deploying.
- **`sanity deploy --dry-run` exists and is genuinely non-publishing** on CLI 8.29. §2's
  acceptance hedges on this; it can be stated.
